{
  "dom_id": "step-q36635",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6xy+{y}^{3}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-6x+3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6y & -6x+3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-6y=0 \\\\ -6x+3{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6y=0\\,⟹\\,x=3y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(3y)+3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ -18y+3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3y(-6+y) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=3y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0 & \\,⟹\\,x=3(0)=0 \\\\ y=6 & \\,⟹\\,x=3(6)=18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(18,6).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(18,6).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points.",
